WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:04.570
NARRATOR: Next, an
orchestra of students

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:07.000
from one of the world's
great science and engineering

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.180
universities comes together
for the world premiere

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:12.750
of a new piece of
music, a tribute

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:16.650
to the events of
the Arab Spring.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:17.690
Meet the composer.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:19.260
SHARIFI: It feels like
actually the tempo of it

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:20.093
slides a little bit.

00:00:20.093 --> 00:00:20.620
HARRIS: Yes.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:21.400
NARRATOR: The conductor.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:22.920
HARRIS: If you end a
phrase with a quarter note,

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.120
ba da ba da da da dee.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:25.340
Hold it full note value.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:26.580
NARRATOR: And the
student performers.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:27.985
ABRAHAM: I thought it
was really phenomenal

00:00:27.985 --> 00:00:29.606
the way he uses
different colors,

00:00:29.606 --> 00:00:31.480
and the way they kind
of play off each other.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.730
I really, really enjoyed it.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:36.200
NARRATOR: The Massachusetts
Institute of Technology

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:46.704
wind ensemble
presents "Awakening."

00:00:46.704 --> 00:00:49.120
JOURNALIST: Activists in Cairo
today pressed their demands

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.410
that Egypt's military rulers
step down immediately.

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:55.180
HARRIS: I can remember
driving to MIT on my commute

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:56.777
and listening to the radio--

00:00:56.777 --> 00:00:59.110
JOURNALIST: Demonstrators say
they'll intensify pressure

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:00.390
for an end to army rule--

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.720
HARRIS: --and
hearing the passion

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.580
in the voices of the people who
were participating in the Arab

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:07.120
Spring.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:08.512
[CHANTING]

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.970
If I could somehow get someone
to write a piece of music that

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:18.910
would have our students have
to think about the Arab Spring

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:22.470
movement, what it means, and
get some historical context

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.520
as it's happening right now.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.270
That was my goal.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:31.460
We are extremely honored
to have an MIT alum back

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.350
on campus, Jamshied Sharifi.

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:34.838
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:36.904 --> 00:01:37.820
SHARIFI: Hey everyone.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:40.028
HARRIS: Jamshied Sharifi is
a New York-based composer

00:01:40.028 --> 00:01:41.670
and producer.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:45.170
He's an artist that is equally
comfortable in the fields

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:49.400
of world music, in pop music,
in composing original music,

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.205
in writing music for film.

00:01:51.205 --> 00:01:52.205
SHARIFI: I'm digging it.

00:01:52.205 --> 00:01:53.663
It sounds really
good in here, too.

00:01:53.663 --> 00:01:56.800
HARRIS: He graduated from
MIT in the mid-1980s.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.460
And he directed, for a number
of years, the MIT Festival Jazz

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:01.370
Ensemble.

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:03.620
So here is someone
who really knows

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:07.140
what it's like to make
music at MIT, how it feels.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.068
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.580
We think of MIT as one of the
world's great institutions

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:23.560
for engineering and
science, but in fact,

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.055
the arts and humanities have
been thriving here for decades.

00:02:27.055 --> 00:02:28.430
And I think it's
something that's

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:31.650
been a part of the fabric
of MIT for many, many years,

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:32.900
but it's not as well known.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.682
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.820
SHARIFI: "Awakening" is
a piece for wind ensemble

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:45.980
that I've written on the
theme of the Arab Spring.

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:51.755
One thing that I
tried to avoid is

00:02:51.755 --> 00:02:53.130
thinking that I
was going to make

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:56.270
any sort of definitive
statement about the Arab Spring.

00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:59.450
Close to two years after
things have happened it's still

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:01.210
a feeling of guarded optimism.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.490
It's not a piece of
traditional Arabic music.

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:09.270
My records neither are
traditional Persian music

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:10.790
or Middle Eastern music.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:12.740
They're a mix of a lot
of different elements,

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:14.573
a lot of things that
I've heard and studied.

00:03:14.573 --> 00:03:16.329
I mean, I had an
education in jazz.

00:03:16.329 --> 00:03:18.245
I spent a lot of time
playing classical music.

00:03:18.245 --> 00:03:20.097
I've played in pop bands.

00:03:20.097 --> 00:03:21.430
I mean, I'm a hybridized person.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:23.390
I'm half Persian
and half American.

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:25.996
So I'm pretty comfortable
mixing those influences.

00:03:25.996 --> 00:03:28.142
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:34.674 --> 00:03:36.590
HARRIS: I've had a fantasy
for quite some time

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:38.710
to have him write for
the MIT Wind Ensemble.

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:41.130
He's never composed
for the idiom

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:44.520
of a wind ensemble, a company of
woodwind, brass, and percussion

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:45.710
players.

00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:49.540
And he's someone who has a
kind of artistic restraint

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:51.990
in his temperament.

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.900
That colors in a very
beautiful way everything

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:55.760
he does in music.

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:03.467
SHARIFI: When you're dealing
with a new piece in front

00:04:03.467 --> 00:04:05.050
of an ensemble, the
first thing you do

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.847
is play it through and let
them make their statement,

00:04:07.847 --> 00:04:09.180
before you have anything to say.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.100
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:16.204 --> 00:04:18.120
ABRAHAM: We re-worked
the ending a little bit.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:20.921
We got to hear specifically
Jamshied talking to Fred,

00:04:20.921 --> 00:04:23.420
and to kind of see those two
come together was a lot of fun.

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:25.545
HARRIS: I think I maybe
went too far the other way,

00:04:25.545 --> 00:04:27.010
so I kind of have
to bring it back.

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:28.426
SHARIFI: It's
better than the ink.

00:04:28.426 --> 00:04:29.430
That's all I can say.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:31.160
JACKSON: Middle Eastern music
has a lot of quarter tones.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:33.600
But when you're writing for
a college wind ensemble,

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:35.683
you can't expect them to
learn how to play quarter

00:04:35.683 --> 00:04:37.330
tones in one concert cycle.

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:39.180
He said for him it was
interesting to have

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:42.441
to figure out how to get that
element of the music in it.

00:04:42.441 --> 00:04:44.440
ADAMS: The baritone sound
is just amazing to me.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:47.180
The trumpet solos,
the woodwind features,

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:50.120
they just all come
together really well.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:53.830
SHARIFI: I've never really
seen the quote unquote

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:56.000
division between the
arts and sciences

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:57.750
as any kind of dichotomy.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:00.840
To be a complete
person, it makes sense

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.620
to be educated in both.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:07.890
So having a vibrant arts
program at a school like MIT

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:10.230
is important for making
complete human beings,

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:12.800
for making human beings
that connect to the world.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:15.125
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:17.962 --> 00:05:19.420
HARRIS: As you
know, there's things

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:22.470
that happen in a concert that
can't happen in a rehearsal.

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:25.310
Everybody brings
their best game.

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:28.130
And also as a listener,
as a composer,

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:29.410
you can really let go.

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:30.520
It's just the concert.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:31.915
This is what it is.

00:05:31.915 --> 00:05:34.050
It's just time to enjoy
it and let it flow.

00:05:40.368 --> 00:05:42.312
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:46.001 --> 00:05:47.500
SHARIFI: The first
movement gives us

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:49.860
a sense of place, which
is the Middle East.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:54.030
And it has a short section
which talks about the event that

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:56.110
sparked the Arab
Spring, which was

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:59.950
the self-immolation
of Mohamed Bouazizi,

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:03.870
and goes on to
musically illustrate

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:08.260
how that event led two uprisings
all over the Middle East.

00:06:11.248 --> 00:06:14.236
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:13:50.590 --> 00:13:56.220
The second movement is a very
reflective and quiet movement

00:13:56.220 --> 00:14:01.240
in which I'm imagining the
participants and observers

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:04.780
of the events of the Spring
just taking stock of what has

00:14:04.780 --> 00:14:08.048
happened, and reflecting on it.

00:14:08.048 --> 00:14:10.543
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:20:19.641 --> 00:20:22.600
And the third movement
is a call to action,

00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:25.430
or a call to continued
action in the hopes

00:20:25.430 --> 00:20:28.270
that what happened
in the Arab Spring

00:20:28.270 --> 00:20:32.935
continues to bring better
political and human situations

00:20:32.935 --> 00:20:33.820
in the Middle East.

00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:36.076
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:25:52.654 --> 00:25:55.059
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:59.390 --> 00:26:02.210
HARRIS: Music is clearly
one of the great innovations

00:26:02.210 --> 00:26:04.890
of humanity, passed
onto us from nature.

00:26:12.130 --> 00:26:15.080
The word awakening kind
of conjures this idea

00:26:15.080 --> 00:26:17.120
that you're coming
forth with something,

00:26:17.120 --> 00:26:20.870
but yet it might not
be fully realized.

00:26:20.870 --> 00:26:23.135
This power of reflection
and contemplation

00:26:23.135 --> 00:26:25.620
is so needed today.

00:26:25.620 --> 00:26:28.380
And I think that
awakening in its own way

00:26:28.380 --> 00:26:29.620
provides a vehicle for that.

00:26:29.620 --> 00:26:32.670
[MUSIC PLAYING]

